{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqqmeewyhir","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Two benches still hand-roll their own judge — SDK-migration holdouts after P4","summary":"P4 finding (2026-06-23): after the P3 de-port, all LOCALLY-RUNNABLE benches that follow the probe/wiring/judge contract already route through the SDK (arms.run_canonical_wiring → POST /judge/drain) — so most \"migration\" was already done by P3. TWO genuine hand-rolled-judge HOLDOUTS remain, NOT yet on the SDK (neither blocks the bench-prod-parity feature): (1) scripts/bench-economy-dag.js — inline judgeEdge() claude -p KEEP/PRUNE rubric (~L271-282) + hard DAG_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.5 fetchDagContext (~L284-312); migrate by subprocessing the Python SDK drain OR injecting raw top-K /search hits and deleting the 0.5 floor — do NOT keep the parallel JS rubric. (2) bench/swe-bench-cl/zonoid_memory.py — ZonoidMemorySystem._llm_judge (~L362) + _judge→POST /judge/verdict (~L524-530); a requests-based EXTERNAL-notebook adapter for the Mac/Docker SWE-bench pilot (not locally runnable); rewrite retrieve_relevant_experience to mint a probe + client.judge_drain, delete _llm_judge/_judge, switch requests→stdlib ZonoidClient — DEFER until that external pilot actually runs. These were emitted as P4 holdouts but the complete() follow_ups array was REFUSED by the daemon (it gates follow_ups to status:done, but the worker handoff mandates status:tested) — a small orchestrator inconsistency worth fixing; captured here so the work isn't lost.","category":null,"tags":["bench","sdk-migration","holdout","feature:bench-prod-parity"],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-23T12:28:15.490Z","vec":[-0.12100739777088165,0.017153190448880196,-0.08265011757612228,-0.0328604094684124,0.04457826167345047,-0.07688412815332413,-0.041012562811374664,0.040532421320676804,-0.026786884292960167,0.054342713207006454,0.026601780205965042,0.029771359637379646,0.012519615702331066,-0.06810283660888672,0.007157203741371632,0.029157016426324844